WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.960
"What Is Titanium Dioxide, and Is It Safe?"

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.880
Food-grade titanium dioxide,
also called E171,

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.640
is used extensively
in food products

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.440
as a whitening agent
in things like chewing gum

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.520
and some maybe
not-so-obvious products,

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.760
like baked goods and candy.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.560
In a survey of foods
from a U.S. grocery store,

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.040
white frostings,
coffee creamers, and chewing gum

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.720
had the highest concentrations,
followed by some candies,

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.960
almond and rice milks,
and dairy products,

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.160
like skim milk, sour cream,
and yogurt.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.520
And the tricky part in the U.S.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.080
is food additives
like titanium dioxide

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.440
are allowed to be listed
generally as "color added"

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.240
or as an anticaking agent
or filler.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.360
So, you may not actually know
what foods contain it.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.480
This is exactly
what the food survey found.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.240
High amounts of titanium dioxide
in some foods with no labeling

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.240
indicating the product
even contained titanium dioxide.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.120
The only reason we care
is because of risk.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.360
Titanium dioxide
has been approved

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.920
as a safe color additive
for use in foods and drugs

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:09.120
in the United States
since 1966

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.480
and the European Union
since 1969,

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:16.560
until the European Food Safety
Authority (known as EFSA)

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.040
concluded in 2021
that titanium dioxide, E171,

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.640
can no longer be considered safe
as a food additive.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.400
What changed?

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.080
Based on all
the evidence available,

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.520
a concern for genotoxicity,
damage to our DNA,

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.600
could not be ruled out.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.040
As a result of this report,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.920
the European Union prohibited
its use as a food additive

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.960
in August 2022.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.280
Since the 2021 report
was issued,

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:44.960
the U.S. Food
and Drug Administration

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.360
has been petitioned
to no longer allow the use

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:51.200
of titanium dioxide in foods,
but that is still pending.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.160
Other international
food regulatory organizations,

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:55.840
like those
in the United Kingdom,

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.160
Canada, Australia,
and New Zealand,

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.040
also do not agree
with EFSA's assessment

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.880
and still allow
titanium dioxide in foods.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.360
Why all the differing opinions?

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.160
The FDA notes that some
of the genotoxicity tests

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.240
considered by EFSA
don't represent

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.960
the titanium dioxide
used in foods.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.600
Food-grade titanium dioxide
is a mixture

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.824
of microsized particles
and nanosized particles.

00:02:21.128 --> 00:02:23.512
Nanoparticles
are tiny materials

00:02:23.512 --> 00:02:27.280
ranging in size
from 1 to 100 nm.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.640
That's about 100,000 times
smaller than a human cell,

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.480
which is what
the genotoxicity studies

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:34.720
in animals were done with.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.760
A common theme voiced
by those disagreeing with EFSA

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.040
is that you can't compare
nanoparticle toxicity studies,

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.520
where researchers mixed
and dispersed the nanoparticles

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:46.800
in a fluid,
with titanium dioxide in foods

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.640
that rely
on bigger microparticles

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.520
for the desired
color appearance.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.040
What about
the 40% nanoparticles?

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:58.160
Well, when you mix nano
and microparticles together,

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.880
a nano might glom
onto a microparticle;

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.800
so, it might not
have the same effect

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.520
as a straight nanoparticle.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:06.640
So, on the one hand,

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.560
the European Union
considers no amount

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.280
of food-grade titanium dioxide
in food to be safe,

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:12.080
banning its use

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.240
and setting off
a worldwide chain reaction

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.760
with bans in other countries

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:19.520
including Switzerland, Qatar,
and most recently, Turkey.

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:20.800
Meanwhile, other countries

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.800
including the U.S.,
UK, and Canada

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.600
don't consider the evidence
for a ban compelling.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.240
DNA damage isn't
the only problem though.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.120
Very little
of the titanium dioxide we eat

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:33.840
is actually absorbed,
but when it is,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.360
it may just sit there
and accumulate over time.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:40.960
But what happens to the 99%
that isn't absorbed?

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.360
It ends up in our colons,
where it can damage colon cells,

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.680
and this
is the food-grade additive.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:50.480
It may also have adverse effects
on our gut microbiome,

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.240
based on studies using just
titanium dioxide nanoparticles,

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:57.440
as well as the mixed version
used as a food additive.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.920
There are nontoxic ways
for food companies

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:03.600
to whiten their products,
like rice starch or rice flour,

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:04.880
but for the same effect,

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.560
more needs to be used,
increasing costs.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.240
If you live in a country
like the U.S.

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:13.440
where it's still legal,
what should you do?

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.920
In my mind, there should
be an extremely high bar

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.320
for safety
for cosmetic additives.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.800
And that's what
titanium dioxide is,

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:23.360
basically white food coloring.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.040
Since it is in no way necessary,

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.920
and tends to be used
in ultra-processed junk food,

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:30.160
we recommend trying to avoid it.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.400
Even if it were
eventually exonerated,

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:33.280
it would still be better

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.000
to use soy milk
than dairy coffee creamer

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.989
and to choose oatmeal
over powdered donuts.

